    "reading": {
        "body": "Seven open AI developer relations and education roles cluster around a small set of employers, with Databricks posting two identical senior developer advocate positions in Seattle and San Francisco.\n\nThe pattern is concentration rather than breadth. Databricks accounts for two of the seven, DirectedLINK two more, and the remaining three come from Lambda, SambaNova, and the U.S. Air Force Academy. Four of the seven sit in California, two in the Bay Area and two in Los Angeles County. Only the Lambda role is marked remote.\n\nThese are teaching and advocacy jobs, not infrastructure jobs. They exist to move developers onto a company's platform, which means they scale with adoption rather than with compute. Nothing here reports on data centers, power contracts, or chip supply, so this data cannot speak to the grid constraint directly. It shows only that a handful of AI platform companies and one federal academy are hiring people to explain their systems to others.\n\nThe sample is seven postings collected on a single day, and it covers only the developer relations and education function. It does not establish a trend, a salary range across the market, or hiring volume over time. Two of the seven listings carry no salary at all, and the two that do come from the same employer.",
